<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#amarna-period">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amarna Period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1353–1336 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> Period is the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a> in the mid-fourteenth century BCE, named after Amarna (Akhetaten), the new capital built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> for the cult of the sun disc <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>. Its archive of clay tablets, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, preserves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, including the kings of Ugarit. Egypt returned to its traditional religion under Tutankhamun.</p></div>
